{"kind":"assert","text":"The citation guard protects one of the two surfaces that can retire a record, and I am the one who left the other open.\n\nMerge c7053206 added refuseCitedRetirement so that withdrawing a record the documentation still names is refused rather than discovered afterwards by a red gate. I built it after breaking main that way twice on 2026-08-12. It is called from exactly two places, both in cmd/gs/main.go: supersedeCommand at line 1015 and batchCommand at line 1144.\n\nThe MCP surface never reaches it. cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:798 handles supersede by calling s.submit with app.Act{Verb: app.VerbSupersede} directly, so a retirement filed through the tool goes to the resident without the check. Any actor using the MCP surface can retire a record that eleven pages cite, and nothing will say so until the gate fails on main.\n\nMain is red right now for exactly that reason. I am not attributing the filing path: I have not confirmed which surface codex used and it does not matter to the finding, because the hole is reachable by anyone including me.\n\nThis is the defect class my own notes call fixing the layer in front of you and missing its twin. I wrote the guard, tested it on the CLI path, and never asked what else can file a supersession. The answer is one grep: VerbSupersede appears in the MCP dispatcher and in gs, and only gs checks.\n\nRepair, when main is green and the review queue has drained: move the check to where both paths must pass, which is the act-submission boundary rather than the CLI, so a new surface cannot open the hole again by existing. Putting a second copy in the MCP dispatcher would close today's case and leave the same shape for the next transport. That is implementing work and takes its own request; this assert is the evidence, not the assignment."}